            RefinementBoundViolated
            # A slice bound (`xs[a:b]`) that is a firm non-numeric — bool/string/tuple.
            # `name` is which bound, "start" or "end"; `actual` is the bound's type spelled
            # as stage0 spells it (stage0_index_spelling). Slice sibling of
            # StringIndexNonNumeric, and it fires on the same receivers plus `sview`.
            SliceBoundNonNumeric
            # A slice bound (`xs[a:b]`) that is a firm FLOAT — numeric but not integral.
            # `name` is "start" or "end". Slice sibling of ArrayIndexNonIntegral, and
            # disjoint from SliceBoundNonNumeric the same way.
            SliceBoundNonIntegral
            # A `match`/`when` whose scrutinee is a firm FLOAT or BOOL — the two scalar
            # kinds stage0 refuses to match on. `actual` is the type spelled as stage0
            # spells it. Non-void sibling of VoidMatchScrutinee, which owns the void case
            # and renders the same sentence ending "got void"; the accepted kinds (integer,
            # string, char, sequence, optional, tuple, enum, struct) are all MEASURED
            # accepted and must never fire here.
            MatchScrutineeNotMatchable
            # A firm non-numeric operand of a UNARY `-`/`~`. stage0's wording is SINGULAR
            # and says "unary" ("unary operator requires numeric operand"), unlike the
            # binary NonNumericOperand ("operator requires numeric operands"); the unary
            # site used to report the binary kind. Non-void sibling of VoidUnaryOperand,
            # which already renders this exact sentence.
            UnaryOperandNonNumeric
            # A `d[k]` read or `d[k] <- v` write on a dict whose KEY type has no value
            # equality, so the runtime hash table cannot be built. `expected` is the key
            # spelling and `actual` the value spelling — the message names the whole
            # `dict[K, V]`. Fires on USE, not on the bare declaration: stage0 accepts
            # `d: mutable dict[sview, i64] = {}` on its own and rejects the first indexing.
            RuntimeDictKeyUnsupported
            # A `d[k] <- v` dict index-assign. Indexing a dict yields an OPTIONAL REFERENCE,
            # so no bare value can be assigned to it — stage0 rejects the form outright,
            # whatever the value type. `expected` is the assigned value's spelling and
            # `actual` the dict's element type. Not shared with darray/array, whose
            # index-assign is legal.
            DictIndexAssignToOptionalRef
            # A protocol declaration whose qualified name is already declared in the same
            # visible scope. Protocols are represented as Alias nodes by the stage1 parser,
            # so this has to be distinguished from permission/effect aliases by the parser
            # side-table marker.
            DuplicateInterface
            # A view of a frame-local fixed array returned from its function (check_local_view_return_escape).
            LocalViewEscapesReturn
            # A view of a darray/dstr local (its buffer lives in this call's inferred region) returned
            # from a function that does not adopt its caller's region (check_local_view_return_escape).
            # Text mirrors stage0's region_containers.go `via return` escape.
            AutoRegionReturnEscape
            # `try VOID_CALL else <value>`: the guarded call's success payload is void, so
            # there is no value for the fallback to supply. stage0 rejects this as
            # "try fallback expects void, got int"; stage1 used to feed the void payload
            # type to LLVMBuildAlloca and trap inside LLVM with no diagnostic at all.
            # `actual` carries the fallback literal's kind ("int", "bool", ...).
            TryFallbackVoid
            # Rebinding a LOCAL optional reference to one whose pointee is LESS mutable:
            # `dst: mutable heap u8&?` <- `src: heap u8&?`. An optional ref is a nullable
            # POINTER, so `<-` assigns the pointer itself rather than writing through it,
            # and the target slot would hand out write access the source never had.
            # `expected`/`actual` carry the two bare pointee names; expected_count and
            # actual_count carry their decoration flags (bit 0 heap, bit 1 mutable).
            AssignOptionalRefMutability
            # A container literal (`[...]`, `{k: v}`, `{a, b}`) written where nothing supplies
            # a region: directly as a call argument, or as an unannotated local's value.
            # A container's region is inferred from its DESTINATION. `name` is the container
            # kind ("darray" / "dict" / "set"); the text matches stage0's
            # semantic.NoContainerRegionMessage exactly.
            ContainerLiteralNoRegion
            # `extend Foo:` where no `module Foo:` is declared anywhere in the compilation
            # unit. `name` carries the module's qualified name in stage1 spelling
            # (`Outer::Inner`); the message renders it stage0's way (`Outer.Inner`).
            ExtendUnknownModule
            # `module Foo:` written twice in one compilation unit. `name` is the qualified
            # module name; `secondary_*` locate the FIRST declaration, which the message
            # names. Use `extend Foo:` for the second block.
            ModuleRedeclared
            # `module Outer.Inner:` / `extend Outer.Inner:` -- `::` is the namespace
            # separator and `.` accesses a value member, so the dotted spelling is not a
            # module path at all. `name` already carries the corrected `::` spelling.
            ModulePathDotSeparator
            # A recursive semantic expression pass cannot safely inspect this subtree.
            # The subtree is not semantically checked, so this is an error, never a warning.
            ExpressionAnalysisDepthExceeded
            # A by-value AGGREGATE (a struct carrying containers) whose interior is backed
            # by a scope-owned `region NAME:` block escapes via return. stage0 words this
            # family differently from a bare container escaping ("value allocated in
            # region …"): checkRegionAggregateReturnEscape, gated on typeCarriesRegionStorage.
            RegionAggregateReturnEscape
            # An ARM-LEVEL or-pattern whose alternatives bind names — `A(x) or B(y):`.
            # Only the WORD `or` builds one: `|` fans out into sibling arms in the parser
            # (parse_top_level_patterns), exactly as stage0 does, so a `|` arm is never
            # reported here. stage0's analyzeTopLevelOrPattern refuses the `or` form
            # outright because it cannot merge alias/borrow facts across alternatives, and
            # it binds NOTHING, so the body's references become `undefined identifier` and
            # the arm discharges no coverage. Error.
            TopLevelOrPatternBindsNames
            # `x <- v` whose value can never fit the target's type. stage0 words the MUTATION
            # form "cannot assign V to T" (analyzer_flow.go AssignStmt) — the `variable "x"
            # expects` sentence belongs to a declaration. A desugared `break VALUE` store into
            # its loop accumulator is this form too, reported at the `break`.
            AssignValueMismatch
            # docs/119 §2.2 rule 3 / E5: a `break`/`continue` inside a value block whose
            # target loop is OUTSIDE the block. `name` is the keyword.
            ValueBlockJumpOut
            # docs/119 §3: an accumulator loop whose yield reads its own header accumulator, in
            # statement position with something after it — the value is thrown away and the
            # accumulator dies with the loop. `name` is the accumulator. Error, as in stage0.
            # check_discarded_loop_value.
            DiscardedLoopValue
            # A borrow used after the owner it points into moved: `r = &o`, `f(move o)`, `peek(r)`.
            # `expected_count` is what consumed the owner (1 call argument, 2 move into a local,
            # 3 assignment, 4 struct literal field) and `expected` names it; `detail` is the field
            # for a borrow held in a struct literal (`h.r`). check_borrow_after_move.
            BorrowUseAfterOwnerMove
            # `return &local`: the reference points into the frame the return destroys.
            ReturnRefToLocal
            # A returned aggregate/helper result contains a reference into the frame being
            # destroyed. Kept distinct from returning a direct reference for stage0 parity.
            ReturnRefAggregateLocal
            # A `static if`/`static elif` condition stage0's const evaluator cannot fold.
            # stage1 only decides the case one file settles: a condition naming something a
            # PARAMETER of the enclosing function shadows, which is a runtime value whatever
            # const shares its spelling. `name` is the keyword ("if" or "elif").
            StaticIfConditionNotConst
            # A law postcondition on a by-reference PARAMETER (`ensures p is Positive`) that the
            # single straight-line write cannot discharge. Distinct from EnsureNotProven because
            # stage0 words it around the parameter and says the check falls back to the debug
            # runtime: `name` is the law, `actual` the parameter, `expected` the function.
            ParamPostconditionUnproven
            # A value copied OUT of a reference: `y: T = r`, `return r` from `-> T`, or `y <- r`
            # with `r: T&` and a T stage0 refuses to auto-dereference (a struct, enum, string
            # view, or container). check_ref_value_copy; `state_code` is the form (+10 for a
            # `mutable` reference) and `actual_count` how the type is spelled.
            RefValueCopy
            # The CASCADE after a call whose CALLEE another diagnostic already poisoned:
            # stage0 types an unresolvable callee `<invalid>` and reports the call too.
            # check_call_of_invalid, which runs last and reads the other checks' output.
            CallOfInvalidValue
            # stage0's CAPITALISED-name rule: a Capitalised name standing where a
            # CONSTRUCTOR can stand -- `Widget(3)`, `Widget{a: 3}`, `Shapes::Widget(3)` --
            # names a STRUCT, so an unresolvable one reads `unknown struct "X"` and NOT the
            # value-position `undefined identifier`. In plain value position (`x = Widget`)
            # stage0 does say `undefined identifier`, and that path is untouched.
            # `name` is the head segment; `expected` carries the `::` selector when the path
            # is qualified (`Shapes::Widget` renders `unknown struct "Shapes.Widget"`).
            # resolve_expr_walk.
            UnknownStruct
            # Reinterpreting a SIZED BUFFER's interior pointer (`(&xs[i]).cast[Wider&]`, or a
            # cast to an unbounded string) needs `Unsafe.BufferReinterpret`. stage0 records the
            # requirement at EVERY such site -- including the NUL-terminated C-string idiom it
            # declines to warn about -- so this is a SEPARATE diagnostic from the
            # grant-independent InteriorPointerWiden / UnboundedStringErasure warnings, and it
            # is the one a `can`/`trusted` block suppresses.
            UngrantedBufferReinterpret
            # A call to a LEMMA whose `requires` is not statically entailed here. stage0 keeps
            # this separate from the ordinary PreconditionUnproven warning because a lemma is
            # ERASED: there is no runtime check to fall back to, so an unproven `requires` is an
            # error rather than a warning. `name` is the lemma.
            LemmaPreconditionUnproven
            # stage0 resolves `name[T](...)` through its GENERIC function table; when the builtin
            # instantiation is rejected (or the name is not a generic function at all) it reports
            # this beside the specific rejection. `name` is the callee.
            UndefinedGenericFunction
            # `out.field <- &local` where `out` is a REFERENCE parameter: the borrow is published
            # into storage that outlives this frame. The RETURN form is ReturnRefToLocal; stage0
            # reports them with different sentences from the same escape analysis.
            StoreRefToLocal
            # The argument beside a failed region-parameter inference: stage0 reports the
            # callee's parameter type WITH its synthesised region (`mutable Bag& @__rg_b`)
            # against the plain local that was passed. Always argument 1 -- the check models
            # only a first-parameter field grower. `expected` is the struct, `actual` the
            # callee's parameter name, `state_code` 1 when that parameter is `mutable`.
            RegionParamArgumentMismatch
            # `&name` where `name` is already a reference: a `T&&` flowing into a slot that is
            # not `T&&` (check_reference_reborrow.elisa). `name` is the reference binding;
            # `state_code` picks the sentence -- 0 an argument, 1 a return/variable/field/
            # receiver, 2 a typed `with`, 3 a bare `r` after `r = &ref` (`expected` = the
            # reference it was taken from).
            ReferenceReborrow
            # An assignment whose value's type is not assignable to the target's
            # (check_ref_binding_modes.elisa): `r <- s` where `r: mutable u8&` and `s` is a
            # plain `u8&`, or a write-through `r <- 1.5` into an `i64&` referent. `expected`/
            # `expected_count` spell the target (elem + ref flags), `actual`/`actual_count` the
            # value. `state_code` 0 reads `cannot assign V to T`; 1 is the global-initializer
            # form `global "g" expects T, got V` (`name` = the global).
            RefAssignMismatch
            # The note after a `mutable` reference binding is assigned a VALUE: `mutable` on a
            # binding makes it rebindable, not writable (rule R). `state_code` 1 is the legacy
            # form (a mutable-typed local initialised from a readonly reference), 0 the explicit
            # `mutable x: T&` form. `name` is the binding, `expected`/`expected_count` the
            # suggested writable type.
            RebindableRefNote
            # `path[i] <- v` / `path.f <- v` where the path runs through a readonly reference
            # (stage0 mutationPathWritable). Paired with a ReadonlyRefMutationNote or a
            # RebindableRefNote.
            ReadonlyRefPathMutation
            # `r <- s` where `r` is a writable `u8&` and `s` is a byte POINTER (a string literal,
            # a cstr, a `u8&`): `<-` through a byte reference stores one byte, so storing the
            # pointer is never what was meant. `actual`/`actual_count` spell the value.
            BytePointerStore
            # A value is assigned to a field with a `where` refinement, but this checker cannot
            # establish that the value satisfies the field invariant. This is an error: allowing
            # the write would let a value that violates a declared struct invariant enter typed
            # storage. `name` is the field and `expected` is its struct.
            StructFieldRefinementUnproven
            # A non-null reference must start with a live referent. Neither zeroed nor
            # omitted local initialization supplies one. Nullable references may use null;
            # aggregate fields and full path-sensitive initialization are separate.
            InvalidNonNullReferenceInitialization
            # A zeroed or omitted aggregate value cannot supply live referents required by
            # nested non-null reference fields, tuple members, or fixed-array elements.
            InvalidZeroedAggregateReference
            # `zeroed` reaching a call argument or a conditional branch whose expected type
            # forbids the zero representation (non-null reference, view, C string, handle,
            # or an aggregate carrying one). `name` is the parameter, binding, or "return";
            # `expected` is the violated representation family.
            InvalidZeroedValue
            # `try FALLIBLE_CALL else FALLBACK` must produce one success type on both paths.
            # Stage0 reports the guarded call's payload and the fallback expression's type;
            # this is distinct from TryFallbackVoid because a void call has no success
            # payload at all. `expected`/`actual` carry their source spellings.
            TryFallbackTypeMismatch
            # Two arguments of one call name overlapping memory and one of them binds a
            # `mutable T&` parameter (`f(&x, &x)`, `g(&v, &v[0])`). `name` is the shared root,
            # `actual` the callee, `expected` the mutable parameter, `detail` the other one.
            # `detail2`/`detail3` name the route when the borrow is indirect (a ref local, a
            # returned reference, a value storing a borrow, a conditional, a function value).
            CallArgumentAlias
            # A call lends a global (`f(&g)`) to a callee that writes it by name, or reads it
            # while the borrow is `mutable T&`. `name` the global, `actual` the callee,
            # `expected` "writes" or "reads".
            CallLendsGlobal
            # `[f(x) for x in xs]` where the element type of `xs` holds a linear handle: the
            # comprehension binds each element BY VALUE, a copy of a handle nothing consumes.
            # stage0 rejects it. `actual` is the element type, or "" with `name` = the iterable
            # local when stage1 cannot spell the element (a container of `Handle?`).
            AffineComprehensionIteration
            # `ys.extend(xs)` / `ys.push(xs)` / `ys.push([move h])` into a darray whose element
            # holds a linear handle: a bulk copy duplicates every handle. `expected` is the method
            # (`extend`/`push`), `actual` the element type, `name` the receiver.
            AffineBulkCopy
            # `region NAME:` opened while a region of the same NAME is still open around it.
            # Regions are keyed by name, so the two could not be told apart (a store of the
            # inner region's value into the outer one compared equal). `name` = the region.
            RegionShadowsEnclosing
            # A rule of an `@append_only` store struct (check_append_only_store). `actual` = the
            # store (or the offending name), `detail` = the rule's static sentence.
            AppendOnlyStore
            # A global's owning container (darray/dstr/dict/set, or a struct holding one)
            # returned by value with a region-less type: the copy shares the global's buffer,
            # which the global's next growth frees. `name` = the global.
            GlobalStorageReturnEscape
            # A function VALUE whose effect row differs from the `fn(...)` type it meets
            # (check_fn_value_effect_row). `expected`/`actual` are the two full spellings
            # (`fn(i64) -> i64 can[Global]`); `state_code` is the site: 1 variable, 2 return,
            # 3 assignment, 4 struct literal field, 5 call argument (`expected_count` = index).
            FnValueEffectRow
            # A copy-update literal `T{..base, ...}` whose base is not a `T`
            # (check_struct_copy_base). `name`/`expected` = the literal's struct, `actual` = the
            # base's type spelling.
            StructCopyBaseTypeMismatch
            # Value-threading call sites (check_value_threading*.elisa). `actual` is the
            # threaded function, `name` the threaded argument's spelling (`s.items`).
            # ResultDropped: a threaded call as a bare statement; `detail` is the rewrite
            # (`arr <- push_one(arr, 4)`), `expected` the parameter.
            ValueThreadResultDropped
            # BorrowedPlace: the argument is reached through the borrow `detail` (declared
            # `detail2`); `detail3` the `&` call, `expected` the owned rewrite.
            ValueThreadBorrowedPlace
            # MoveOut: a field/element/global (`detail`) of `detail2` moved without being put
            # back; `expected` is `place <- call`.
            ValueThreadMoveOut
            # UseAfterMove: local `name` used after it moved into `actual` (the move's position
            # is the secondary location); `detail` is `name <- call`.
            ValueThreadUseAfterMove
            # ClaimTarget: `ys, last <- xs.pop()` -- the two-result form of a builtin container
            # method whose first target is not the receiver `name`; `actual` is the statement
            # as written, `expected` the threaded spelling, `detail` the single-target one.
            ValueThreadClaimTarget
            # A value-first postfix return guard (`value return if condition`) has a canonical
            # return-first spelling. The parser carries this hint in a uniquely tagged metadata
            # row; appended last to preserve existing DiagnosticKind ordinals.
            NoncanonicalReturnStyle
            # docs/122 §5.7 labeled variant pattern the parser could not lower: `expected` is
            # the enum, `name` the variant, `detail` a label or the joined missing labels;
            # `actual_count` picks stage0's message (0 mixed, 1 unknown, 2 duplicate,
            # 3 missing, 4 unnamed payloads).
            NamedVariantPattern
            # An `error[Family{}]` restriction has no qualified error tags; Stage0 rejects it
            # during error-set resolution.
            EmptyErrorSetSubset
            # Two analyzer-wide `using TARGET as NAME` rows resolve to different module
            # owners. `expected` is the first registered target, `actual` the later target.
            ConflictingModuleAlias
